# Multi-Branch Enterprise Network Lab (Cisco Packet Tracer)

A simulated two-site enterprise network for a fictional apparel manufacturer, **Fashionista (Pvt) Ltd**, with a head office in **Colombo** and a new branch in **Kegalle**. Designed, addressed (VLSM), configured and tested in Cisco Packet Tracer.

> This is a personal lab project based on a simulated scenario. It is not a production or real-client network.

![Topology](diagrams/topology.png)

---

## Scenario

| Site | Floors | Main areas |
|---|---|---|
| Colombo (HQ) | 3 | Reception/Customer Services (Wi-Fi), Designers, Maintenance, Administration, Manufacturing, Directors, Boardroom (video conferencing + Wi-Fi), HR (fingerprint scanner), Accounting & Finance, IT, Server Room |
| Kegalle (branch) | 2 | Reception, Customer Services (Wi-Fi), Manufacturing (2 embroidery plants), 14 IP cameras, Administration, HR (fingerprint scanner), Accounting & Finance, IT, Server Room |

### Requirements

- Every department on its own subnet/VLAN
- Secured Wi-Fi in the Boardroom and Customer Services areas
- Inter-branch connectivity with inter-VLAN routing and a dynamic routing protocol
- Own IP range with VLSM subnetting (10.1.0.0/16)
- All PCs obtain addresses from a DHCP server
- Server room on **20.0.0.0/24** with static IPs

---

## Design Summary

- **Topology:** hierarchical star at each site (Layer 3 core switch, floor access switches), point-to-point WAN link between the branch routers
- **Addressing:** VLSM from `10.1.0.0/16`. Colombo uses `10.1.0.0/24`, Kegalle uses `10.1.1.0/24`
- **VLANs:** 20 in total (11 Colombo departments + 1 server VLAN + 8 Kegalle)
- **Routing:** inter-VLAN routing on L3 core switches (SVIs), RIP v2 (`no auto-summary`, passive interfaces) between sites
- **DHCP:** one central server in Colombo, with `ip helper-address` relay on every SVI (including Kegalle, over the WAN)
- **Services:** DNS, Web (HTTP), Email (SMTP/POP3), File (FTP), all in `20.0.0.0/24`
- **Security:** WPA2-PSK (AES) on Wi-Fi, SSH v2 for device management, native VLAN 1 on all trunks, VLAN separation per department

### Design decision and trade-off

All application servers are centralised at Colombo. This lowers Kegalle's cost and administration effort, but Kegalle depends on the WAN link for DHCP, DNS, email and file access. A backup WAN or VPN link is the first recommended enhancement.

